cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Testing the Fluorescence Advantage: Solar-Induced Fluorescence as an Early Indicator of Agricultural Drought Stress in Marathwada, Maharashtra"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/GREEN_ALIBI"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/GREEN_ALIBI"
abstract: >-
  A satellite remote-sensing study testing whether Solar-Induced Fluorescence
  (SIF), a proxy for photosynthetic activity, registers agricultural drought
  stress measurably earlier than NDVI, the reflectance-based indicator
  currently underpinning India's drought declaration and PMFBY crop-insurance
  payout process. Using GOSIF v2 fluorescence data and cloud-screened MODIS
  NDVI over Marathwada, Maharashtra, across two drought years (2015, 2018)
  and one normal monsoon year (2020), a quantitative SIF-to-NDVI decline lag
  was calculated and independently corroborated by a second, methodologically
  distinct cross-correlation lag estimate and a case-resampling bootstrap
  uncertainty analysis. SIF's decline preceded NDVI's decline consistently
  across all three years studied, but the hypothesis that drought severity
  amplifies this lag was not supported. Independent CHIRPS rainfall
  validation confirmed the drought/normal classification, and district-level
  spatial analysis found mean SIF and rainfall anomaly strongly and
  significantly correlated (Pearson r = 0.837, p < 0.001), a relationship
  further characterized via a Moran's I spatial-autocorrelation diagnostic.
  A supplementary comparison against the one well-documented official
  government drought-declaration date available (2018) found both satellite
  indicators leading the official declaration by roughly seven weeks.
keywords:
  - solar-induced fluorescence
  - remote sensing
  - drought monitoring
  - NDVI
  - agricultural stress
  - Marathwada
  - Google Earth Engine
  - crop insurance
  - PMFBY
  - spatial statistics
